<!-- Exercise 035 - ScrollViewerAndSizing (beginner).
     Goal:   Put over-tall content inside a fixed-height ScrollViewer so it
             genuinely overflows, and separately clamp a Border with
             MinWidth/MaxHeight against a Width/Height that fight them.
     Drills: ScrollViewer, MinWidth/MaxHeight interaction.
     Passes: dotnet test, filtered to Ex035_ (see the exact command in
     Ex035_ScrollViewerAndSizing.axaml.cs - an XML comment may not hold two
     hyphens in a row, so it cannot appear here). -->
<UserControl xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             x:Class="FeWoLearning.Avalonia.Exercises.Beginner.Ex035_ScrollViewerAndSizing">
  <!-- TODO: replace this placeholder with:
         <StackPanel Spacing="8">
           <ScrollViewer Name="Scroller" Height="60"
                         HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Disabled">
             <StackPanel>
               <TextBlock Text="Line 1" Height="40" />
               <TextBlock Text="Line 2" Height="40" />
               <TextBlock Text="Line 3" Height="40" />
               <TextBlock Text="Line 4" Height="40" />
               <TextBlock Text="Line 5" Height="40" />
               <TextBlock Text="Line 6" Height="40" />
               <TextBlock Text="Line 7" Height="40" />
               <TextBlock Text="Line 8" Height="40" />
             </StackPanel>
           </ScrollViewer>
           <Border Name="Clamped" MinWidth="250" MaxHeight="40"
                   Width="50" Height="200" Background="#EEEEEE" />
         </StackPanel>
       The eight 40px rows total 320px inside a ScrollViewer pinned to 60px
       tall, so Scroller.Extent.Height ends up well above Scroller.Viewport.Height
       - a fixed-height Border with no ScrollViewer at all cannot reproduce
       that pair of numbers, because it has neither an Extent nor a Viewport.
       HorizontalScrollBarVisibility is Disabled because nothing here
       overflows horizontally - a vertical-only scroller keeps the assertion
       about Extent/Viewport unambiguous. Clamped's own Width (50) loses to
       its larger MinWidth (250), and its own Height (200) loses to its
       smaller MaxHeight (40) - assert its resulting Bounds. -->
  <StackPanel>
    <TextBlock Text="TODO" />
  </StackPanel>
</UserControl>
